;; Docs for std/simd.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "simd.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Vectors of numbers, 128 or 256 bits wide, with one CPU instruction per operation.")
  (see Simd SimdBitwise SimdMask SimdFloat SimdInt SimdBits SimdWiden SimdNarrow SimdSource Mask256 simd256-load simd256-whole simd-mask-bits simd256-accelerated? Float64x4 Int32x4)
  (example """
    (import (std simd))

    ;; The dot product of two double arrays, four lanes at a time.
    (: dot (-> (Array double) (Array double) double))
    (defun (dot a b)
      (def w (simd256-lanes a))
      (def whole (simd256-whole a))
      (def acc
        (loop (:for i (up-from 0 #:to whole #:by w))
              (:acc v (folding (simd256-zero)
                               (simd-add v (simd-mul (simd256-load a i) (simd256-load b i)))))
              => v))
      ;; The elements after the last whole vector, one at a time.
      (+ (simd-sum acc)
         (loop (:for i (up-from whole #:to (array-length a)))
               (:acc s (folding 0.0 (+ s (* (array-ref a i) (array-ref b i)))))
               => s)))
    """)
  (reference """
    A lane type is a .NET @code{Vector128<T>} or @code{Vector256<T>}: a value
    that holds 128 or 256 bits of numbers of one type, in lanes. The names
    say the element and the number of lanes: @code{Float64x4} is four
    doubles, @code{Int8x32} is 32 signed bytes. @code{(Simd256 double)} is the
    same type as @code{Float64x4}.

    @strong{Operations.} The operations are the methods of a few traits,
    which both widths implement, so @code{simd-add} adds two
    @code{Float64x2} values or two @code{Int32x8} values. At a known type,
    each call compiles to the .NET intrinsic, which the JIT compiles to one
    instruction. Arithmetic on integer lanes wraps.

    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{Simd}: what every element type has. Arithmetic, min and
        max, comparisons, @code{simd-select}, sums, and one lane.}
      @item{@code{SimdBitwise}: and, or, xor, not and and-not, for vectors
        and for masks.}
      @item{@code{SimdMask}: reading a mask.}
      @item{@code{SimdFloat}: what only float lanes have. Division, square
        root, rounding, NaN and fused multiply-add.}
      @item{@code{SimdInt}: what only integer lanes have. Shifts,
        saturating arithmetic, and shuffles.}
      @item{@code{SimdLookup}: 16-byte table lookups, a vector of bytes at
        a time.}
      @item{@code{SimdBits}: the same bits read as another element type.}
      @item{@code{SimdWiden}, @code{SimdNarrow}, @code{SimdIntToFloat} and
        @code{SimdFloatToInt}: conversions to another element type.}
    }

    @strong{Making a vector.} The functions that make a vector say their
    width in the name, because there is no vector argument to take it from:
    @code{simd256-load}, @code{simd128-splat}, @code{simd256-zero},
    @code{(simd128-of 1 2 3 4)}. The element type comes from the argument,
    or from where the vector is used. A conversion makes a vector of another
    type, and says its width too.

    @strong{Sources.} @code{simd128-load} and @code{simd256-load} read from
    any @code{SimdSource}: an array or a @code{MutableVec}.

    @strong{Masks.} A comparison such as @code{simd-lt} gives a mask, a
    @code{(Mask128 %a)} or @code{(Mask256 %a)}: every bit of a lane is set
    where the comparison holds and none where it does not. A mask is a type
    of its own, so it can not be added up or multiplied by mistake.
    @code{simd-and}, @code{simd-or} and @code{simd-not} combine masks.
    @code{simd-select}, @code{simd-any?}, @code{simd-all?},
    @code{simd-count-true} and @code{simd-first-true} read one.
    @code{simd-mask-bits} gives an int with one bit per lane, which
    @code{trailing-zeros} from @code{(std maths)} walks: that is how a search
    finds every match in a vector, not only the first.
    @code{simd-mask->lanes} gives the lanes themselves, -1 where set and 0
    where not for integer lanes, for code that counts matches by subtracting
    them.

    @strong{Floats and integers.} @code{SimdFloat} and @code{SimdInt} are
    implemented only for float and only for integer lanes, so a shift of a
    @code{Float64x4} or the square root of an @code{Int32x8} is refused when
    the program is compiled. Integer division is not offered: there is no
    instruction for it, and a zero divisor would raise.

    @strong{Conversions.} Each name says what happens to a value that does
    not fit. @code{simd128-widen-lower} and @code{-upper} widen half the
    lanes to twice the bits, sign-extending a signed lane and zero-extending
    an unsigned one. @code{simd128-narrow-wrapping} and
    @code{-saturating} make one vector of half the bits from two.
    @code{simd128-int->float} rounds to the nearest float.
    @code{simd128-float->int-truncating} goes toward zero, saturates past
    the range, and gives 0 for NaN. @code{simd-bits->int64} and its family
    keep the bits and read them as another type.

    @strong{Moving lanes.} Every other operation works lane by lane:
    @code{simd-shuffle} is how a value reaches another lane, as in reversing
    or reordering. @code{simd-lookup} uses the bytes of a vector as indices
    into a table of 16: sixteen or thirty-two lookups in one instruction,
    which is how fast text code classifies and converts bytes. Neighbouring
    elements are compared by loading again one element on, at
    @code{(+ i 1)}: an unaligned load is as fast as an aligned one.

    @strong{Element types.} A vector holds one of the primitive numbers
    that the trait @code{Lane} lists. A vector of anything else, such as
    @code{(Simd128 char)}, can be named as a type, but nothing makes or
    takes one: the checker refuses it.

    @strong{Design.} The library follows OxCaml's SIMD library where that
    fits a language with traits and unsigned types: masks of their own
    type, operations only for the element types they mean something for,
    and conversions named for what they do. Unlike OxCaml's, it has
    unsigned lane types, so a comparison or a widening of bytes is unsigned
    by the type, not by the name of the operation.

    @strong{The tail.} An array whose length is not a multiple of the lanes
    has elements after the last whole vector. @code{simd256-whole} is the
    index where they start; handle them one at a time, as in the example.
    @code{simd256-load} raises @code{ArgumentOutOfRangeException} when a
    whole vector does not fit. @code{simd256-whole} takes any source.

    @strong{Which width.} A 128-bit vector is accelerated on every x86-64
    and ARM64 CPU. A 256-bit vector needs AVX2; elsewhere .NET emulates it,
    which is much slower than scalar code. @code{simd256-accelerated?} says
    which. A Native AOT build targets a CPU without AVX2 unless the manifest
    says @code{(aot-cpu "native")} or a level such as @code{"x86-64-v3"}.

    @strong{Speed.} Use more than one accumulator for a sum: an addition
    waits for the one before it to finish. On 4 million doubles with AVX2,
    a dot product with four @code{Float64x4} accumulators took 1.4 ms, and a
    scalar loop 9.9 ms. For the common reductions, @code{(std array)} has
    @code{array-sum} and @code{array-dot}, which do this already.
    """))

(:doc Simd128
  (summary "A 128-bit vector of numbers of type %a: .NET's @code{Vector128<T>}.")
  (tparam %a "The element type: a primitive number, one of @code{Lane}.")
  (see Simd simd128-load simd128-splat))

(:doc Simd256
  (summary "A 256-bit vector of numbers of type %a: .NET's @code{Vector256<T>}.")
  (tparam %a "The element type: a primitive number, one of @code{Lane}.")
  (see Simd simd256-load simd256-splat))

(:doc Lane
  (summary "The element types a vector holds: the primitive numbers.")
  (tparam %a "The element type.")
  (see IntLane Simd)
  (reference """
    @code{double}, @code{System.Single}, @code{long}, @code{int},
    @code{short}, @code{System.SByte}, @code{ulong}, @code{uint},
    @code{ushort} and @code{byte}. It has no methods: it only says which
    types these are, so that a vector of another type, which .NET refuses
    at run time, is refused when the program is compiled. Every operation
    and every way to make a vector requires it.
    """))

(:doc IntLane
  (summary "The integer element types, for the operations only integer lanes have.")
  (tparam %a "The element type.")
  (see Lane FloatLane SimdInt)
  (reference """
    The eight integer types of @code{Lane}. @code{SimdInt} requires it.
    """))

(:doc FloatLane
  (summary "The float element types, @code{double} and @code{System.Single}.")
  (tparam %a "The element type.")
  (see Lane IntLane SimdFloat))

(:doc Mask128
  (summary "The answer of a comparison of two 128-bit vectors of %a: which lanes it holds for.")
  (tparam %a "The element type of the vectors compared.")
  (see SimdMask SimdBitwise simd-select Mask256)
  (reference """
    It holds a @code{(Simd128 %a)} with every bit of a lane set where the
    comparison holds, and none where it does not. Only @code{(std simd)}
    makes one. @code{simd-mask->lanes} gives the vector.
    """))

(:doc Mask256
  (summary "The answer of a comparison of two 256-bit vectors of %a: which lanes it holds for.")
  (tparam %a "The element type of the vectors compared.")
  (see SimdMask SimdBitwise simd-select Mask128))

(:doc SimdBitwise
  (summary "The bitwise operations, of vectors and of masks.")
  (tparam %v "A vector or a mask type.")
  (see simd-and simd-or simd-xor simd-not simd-and-not))

(:doc SimdMask
  (summary "Reading a mask: which lanes are set.")
  (tparam %m "A mask type, @code{(Mask128 %a)} or @code{(Mask256 %a)}.")
  (see simd-any? simd-all? simd-count-true simd-first-true simd-mask-bits simd-mask->lanes))

(:doc SimdFloat
  (summary "The operations only float lanes have.")
  (tparam %v "A vector of @code{FloatLane} elements, such as @code{Float64x4}.")
  (see simd-div simd-sqrt simd-floor simd-ceiling simd-round simd-truncate simd-nan? simd-mul-add))

(:doc SimdInt
  (summary "The operations only integer lanes have.")
  (tparam %v "A vector of @code{IntLane} elements, such as @code{Int32x8}.")
  (see simd-shift-left simd-shift-right simd-shift-right-logical simd-add-saturating simd-sub-saturating simd-shuffle IntLane))

(:doc SimdLookup
  (summary "Table lookups: each byte of a vector picks one of 16 bytes.")
  (tparam %v "The vector of indices: @code{UInt8x16} or @code{UInt8x32}.")
  (see simd-lookup ByteLane))

(:doc ByteLane
  (summary "The element type of @code{simd-lookup}'s indices: @code{byte}.")
  (tparam %a "The element type.")
  (see SimdLookup))

(:doc SimdBits
  (summary "The same bits, read as lanes of another type.")
  (tparam %v "The vector type.")
  (see simd-bits->int64 simd-bits->float64 simd-bits->uint8))

(:doc SimdWiden
  (summary "Widening, by the element type: half the lanes, each to twice the bits.")
  (tparam %a "The element type of the vector widened.")
  (see simd128-widen-lower simd128-widen-upper simd256-widen-lower simd256-widen-upper SimdNarrow)
  (reference """
    @code{System.SByte} to @code{short}, @code{byte} to @code{ushort},
    @code{short} to @code{int}, @code{ushort} to @code{uint}, @code{int} to
    @code{long}, @code{uint} to @code{ulong}, and @code{System.Single} to
    @code{double}. Every value fits: a signed lane is sign-extended and an
    unsigned one zero-extended.
    """))

(:doc SimdNarrow
  (summary "Narrowing, by the element type: two vectors into one of half the bits a lane.")
  (tparam %a "The element type of the vectors narrowed.")
  (see simd128-narrow-wrapping simd128-narrow-saturating SimdWiden)
  (reference """
    @code{short} to @code{System.SByte}, @code{ushort} to @code{byte},
    @code{int} to @code{short}, @code{uint} to @code{ushort}, @code{long}
    to @code{int} and @code{ulong} to @code{uint}.
    """))

(:doc SimdIntToFloat
  (summary "Integer lanes to float lanes of the same bits.")
  (tparam %a "The element type converted: @code{int}, @code{uint}, @code{long} or @code{ulong}.")
  (see simd128-int->float simd256-int->float SimdFloatToInt))

(:doc SimdFloatToInt
  (summary "Float lanes to integer lanes of the same bits, toward zero.")
  (tparam %a "The element type converted: @code{System.Single} or @code{double}.")
  (see simd128-float->int-truncating simd128-float->uint-truncating SimdIntToFloat))

(:doc Simd
  (summary "What every vector has, implemented by @code{Simd128} and @code{Simd256} at every @code{Lane} type.")
  (tparam %v "The vector type, such as @code{Float64x4}.")
  (see simd-add simd-lt simd-select simd-sum SimdBitwise SimdFloat SimdInt))

